WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.840
Привет, Датмин Схалуэн Рашен.

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.420
Уэлком тупривет русский, эписот 47.

00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:08.240
По 22 июня 2020 года.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:09.560
I'm Olya.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.200
Today's lesson is going to be a fun one.

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:13.240
Поехали.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.300
Today's word of the day is sabaka.

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:17.760
Sabaka.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.600
Датминс док.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.540
Сенького сайбирин-хаски, and bark, the word out loud.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.400
It is easy to remember because the sound feels like a friendly bark.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:28.900
Repeat after me.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:29.900
Са бака.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.620
Now listen to it in a sentence.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:34.780
Моя sabaka большая.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.020
That means my dog is big.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:39.260
We are exploring animal study.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.820
So here are more words to help you talk about creature around us.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.180
The next word is кошка.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.340
кошка.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:47.580
That means cat.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.000
Repeat after me.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:49.900
кошка.

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.260
Пикчер аккет, корол даплоко кушен, and the word will stick.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.140
кошка спит на ковре.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:57.980
The cat is sleeping on the rug.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:02.680
The memory hook works because кошка sounds like kusham.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:07.360
So you can imagine the cat resting on one while you practice the soft Osh.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.320
Sound in the middle, say the fourth rest slowly first, then at natural speed.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:17.220
So the ending K stays clear and matches the feminine pattern we will discuss later.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.480
Now that you know кошка, let's meet птица.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:21.860
Птица.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.380
That means bird.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:24.460
Repeat after me.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:25.040
Птица.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.060
It flies through the sky like a tiny tweed.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.360
Птица пает в небе.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.180
The bird sinks in the sky.

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:37.120
The memory hook connects птица to the idea of a petite tweed.

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:43.660
Because the quick Pтица part feels light, and the Chesa ending flatters like wings.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:50.520
Practice the soft T sound, so it does not turn into hard D, then repeat the whole sentence

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:52.680
while picturing the board high above.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:54.940
Next comes рыба.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:55.900
Орюба.

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.540
That means fish.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:58.700
Repeat after me.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:01:59.480
Рыба.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:03.440
Imagine a fish with a ribbon tail swimming in the water.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.160
Рыба плавает в воде.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.080
The fish swim in the water.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:11.420
The memory hook works because рыба rhymes with ribbon.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.740
Let's meet a picture the tail streaming behind like fabric while the fish moves through the water.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.460
Say the Russian sentence twice.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.020
First slowly to hear the real start.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.760
Then at normal speed.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.940
So the bar ending clans cleanly.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:27.980
Remember Sloan from before.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.740
Sloan still means elephant.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:31.560
Our biggest animal friend.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.720
Sloan большой серый.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:35.580
The elephant is big and gray.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:40.700
The memory hook reminds you that Sloan remains the word for the largest land animal.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.920
So the single syllable Sloan.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.060
Stay short and heavy like the animal itself.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.940
Repeat the word then the full sentence.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:55.200
Noticing that the masculine Indian gives the adjective bolshoi unchanged in the simple description.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.360
Remember Zayets too.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.220
Zayets still means hair.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:01.580
The speedy jumper.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.980
Zayets preguit быстро.

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.540
The hair jumps fast.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.620
The memory hook recalls Zayets as our quick friend from earlier lessons.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.920
With the Zayets start sounding like a sudden leap.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.420
And the ads ending snapping shut like a sprint.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.520
Say the word first.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.600
Or then speak the full sentence while imagining the hair bounding across open ground.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:25.820
Here is live.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:26.860
Live.

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:28.860
That means lion.

00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:30.480
Repeat after me.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:31.120
Live.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:35.520
The sound is soft like a leaf where a lion might hide.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:36.320
Live.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:37.600
Receive gromka.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.180
The lion roars loudly.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.540
The memory hook links live to leave.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:45.680
So you can picture the lion resting among jungle leaves.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:47.320
And the soft lie start.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.240
Helps you keep the F at the end gentle.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:54.660
Repeat both a single word and the sentence, listening for the short vowel.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.920
That makes the roar feel less harsh.

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:58.960
Now we reach mid-vid.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:00.580
Mid-vid.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.500
That means bear.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:03.780
Repeat after me.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.000
Mid-vid.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.900
It loves honey which makes the word easy to picture.

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.140
Mid-vid loves meat.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.160
The bear loves honey.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:17.820
The memory hook breaks mid-vid into honey noir.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:23.480
Show the how the bear's name points directly to its favorite food without using any fiercer term.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:26.060
Finally, we have a lochet.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:27.400
A lochad.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.200
That means horse.

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.360
Repeat after me.

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:31.240
Lochet.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.340
It gallops across fields.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:34.900
At locomotive speed.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:35.700
Lochet.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:36.600
Bежит по полю.

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:38.880
The horse runs across the field.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:43.280
The memory hook ties lochet to locomotive speed.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:47.340
So the steady rhythm of the word matches the steady gallop.

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:50.040
You can hear in your mind while saying the full sentence.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.920
Russian nouns have secret labels called genders that change how words work.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.540
Most animal names ending in the sound are feminine.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.000
Like sabaka and koshka.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.100
Words like slone and leaf are masculine.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:08.820
English does not mark nouns this way.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.860
But in Russian, the label tells you how to say my or big with the right ending.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:18.220
Now you can say my yes sabaka or my slone correctly.

00:05:18.920 --> 00:05:21.760
The same rule applies to the new words we just learned.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:25.700
Famine ending stays soft while my screen won't stay form.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.700
In Russian fairy tales,

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.980
the bear is often a wise but clumsy forest uncle.

00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.040
Children hear stories where midviet helps or tricks other animals.

00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:41.060
The word feels like an old friend from the woods rather than just a zoo animal.

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.740
This tradition tors the name into Sunday familiar

00:05:44.740 --> 00:05:48.000
that appears in many bad time tales passed down to families.

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:52.160
Now here is something really cool about one of today's words.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.540
Want to know a secret about midviet?

00:05:55.540 --> 00:06:00.460
Did you know that Russian word midviet and the English idea of a honey-eater

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.540
share the same ancient foot?

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:08.060
Long ago, people used this polite name instead of the animals real scary name.

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.320
The word traveled through all Slavic roots and stayed in Russian

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.340
while English chose the fiercest sounding bear.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:19.040
Both languages hid the true name out of respect.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:23.520
Next time you see honey, remember it is cousins with midviet.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:26.180
They both come from the same sweet secret.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.680
It is time for our practice challenge.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:33.720
If you are a kid, draw five animals from today and label each one in Russian.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:36.640
Then roar or meow, the word out loud.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.920
If you are an adult, walk around your home or neighborhood

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:43.460
and name every animal you see using the new words.

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:46.720
Say at a Koska or at a Ptitsa as you go.

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.420
Maladets, that means well done.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:52.940
Remember every expert started as a beginner.

00:06:52.940 --> 00:06:56.960
Practice saying today's words out loud, even just once.

00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:59.080
And you'll be amazed how fast you learn.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.000
See you next time poka.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:02.500
That's Russian for bye.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:07.860
And if you'd rather watch than listen, find us on YouTube at NERA-RU

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.540
links in the show notes.

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:12.940
At that выпуск as Putin's помощью EE synthesis голоса

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:16.600
под борцем и анализ остается за человеком.
